From: pcm@world.std.com (Peter C. McCluskey)
Date: Thu, 19 May 1994 10:06:01 -0400
Subject: [#94-5-228] Rights of Orangutangs

 freeman@netcom.com  (Jay Reynolds Freeman) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-5-220:
>  This line of reasoning leads to interesting statments about "rights" and
>other matters, such as "so-and-so wasn't brought up right, so now he isn't
>human", which is not too far from the legal position whereby certain
>convicted criminals are deprived of some "rights", and "he isn't human now,
>but maybe we can fix it by corrective counseling."  In the line of reasoning
>about "natural rights" that has them behaviorally based and accepts that
>behavior is influenced by environment, each of these statements may have
>merit.

 Laws (including the definition of who has rights) need to be kept as
simple as possible in order to minimize:
  a) the bugs in them,
  b) the misunderstandings about what the law means which allow people to
	bend them, and
  c) the conflicts about what the law ought to say.
Trying to define "human" is an unnecessary complication here.

 The dangerously slippery slopes that the FES talks about can be avoided
if we think in terms of contract violations rather than try to define
"rights" or "criminals".
 If person X injures entity Y (which may be a person, orangutang, rock, or
software process), the questions to ask are: Does Y demand compensation
for the injury, and if X fails to pay compensation, does Y take action
to convince the world that X is in default on X's obligations, or get
someone else to convince the world of that.
 The question of whether Y has rights then becomes: can Y communicate
a breach-of-contract complaint to society?

