From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Thu, 19 May 94 21:00:52 GMT
Subject: [#94-5-233] insurance, risk-pools, freedom

Brian Barnhart writes:
>For some time I've ben trying to reconcile the coerciveness of insurance
>with a [perceived] need for risk-tolerance, and wanted to hear
>extropian views. IN particular, I'm wondering about govm't-mandated
>insurance and requirements.
>
>Some favorite examples are: auto insurance and seat-belts, motorcycle
>insurance and helmets, general health insurance and high-risk behavior.
>
>As one comfortable with seat belts, I do have a problem in subsidizing
>those whose faces have passed through a windshield.

As a signed-up cryonicist who aims to live forever, the very last thing I
want is to be trussed up like a chicken in a blazing wreck, and burnt
beyond all recognition.  I have a problem with establishment speculators
who come up with some pseudoscientific theory, and then use force to
coerce others into practising the officially prescribed "low-risk"
behavior.  And all because of socialised medicine.

People accept a certain degree of risk in their lifestyles, and if some
particular risk is perceived to have decreased, the people will then
adjust behavior until the risk has returned to its previous value,
hence increasing reward.  So since the wearing of seat belts causes
drivers to feel more secure, they drive more dangerously, hitting more
pedestrians and cyclists, and cause more accidents.  Laws imposing
the wearing of seat belts have the effect of redistributing casualties,
saving a few drunk drivers for example whilst killing and injuring many
innocent third parties.

Once socialised medicine has been imposed on the people, governments may
then use "risk-reduction" and "cost-cutting" as the excuses to launch
further attacks on freedom.  Extropianism and libertarianism are
diametrically opposed to coercion and national unhealth services.

>One proposal might
>be to have risk-pools, like exist for non-drinking drivers. Another
>might be to make coverage proportional to risk-exposure: eg, seat-belted
>claimants are covered 100%, no-seat-belts covered 40%.

In a truly free society, no particular side of an argument would have
special privileges in terms of media coverage.  In such a situation, if
private insurance companies tended to charge higher premiums for certain
behavior I couldn't complain if some of my choices were deemed "high-risk";
at least with competitive insurance some companies would take an
alternative view and might even reverse some of the rankings.

>Then, as a friend pointed out, so many folks have no insurance at all
>that we end up subsidizing them anyway. Hence laws requiring insurance,
>and laws regarding behavior on 'public' thoroughfares.

Third party insurance is another issue, and I do think that everyone
should be liable for the consequences of their actions.  If folk failed
to insure themselves and consequently were unable to compensate their 
victims, they should be forced to work to pay off the debt, even if it
meant 50 years' hard labor.  In this instance the force is retaliatory
rather than initiatory.

Steve Whitrow                           sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk

