From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Date: Fri, 20 May 1994 00:25:16 -0400
Subject: [#94-5-238] Jungle ethics

Ian Douglas <iandoug@cybernet.za> asks:
 
> What rights does a lion grant to a deer?
 
   I once suggested that if people are so concerned about cruelty to
animals, they should pay attention to the unspeakable crimes animals
commit to each other.
   If you have any concern about animals' suffering, you should talk 
about establishing jungle police.
 
   Somehow people don't like such arguments though, just as they didn't
like my suggestions to generalize the abortion arguments for alternative
reproducton techniques.
  E.g., what would people defining the right of a woman for an abortion
as her right to control her body  say if humans were laying eggs (which
takes the fetus out of the mother's body and leaves the issue of respon-
sibility) or spawning caviar (does every one of a few million pieces of
caviar have a sacred right to life that should be realized at all costs?)
 
   Looks like all ethical principles are extremely subjective, and the
scarce attempts ever made to rationalize them usually are directly aimed
at pursuing some egotistic goals.
 
   So, with all my interests in building transhuman ethics on a firm
philosophical foundation, I would expect that the social ethics will
continue to be formed by memetic power struggle with little respect
to reason.
 
- Sasha.
 
